Two acronyms, both meaning "tax withheld before you get paid" — and both regularly confused for each other. Here's the difference, in plain terms.

## APIT: withheld from your salary

Advance Personal Income Tax is withheld by your employer directly from your pay, based on your expected annual employment income. It's an estimate collected in instalments throughout the year, reconciled against your actual final tax bill when you file.

## AIT: withheld from investment income

Advance Income Tax applies to certain investment income — interest on fixed deposits is the most common case — withheld by the bank or institution paying you, at the point of payment.

## Why the distinction matters

Both are credits against what you actually owe, not extra taxes on top. If your circumstances mean less tax is due than what was withheld under either scheme, that difference is refundable. Checking both figures against your final computation is the only way to know whether you're owed money back.
